  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a proposed pathway for building chiral membrane-like vesicles from mixed phospholipids and small amphiphiles. Blue and red L- and D-phospholipids first assemble into a vesicle in solvent, while smaller blue and yellow amphiphiles remain more dispersed. Inside this environment, the amphiphiles appear to cluster into a small aggregate or micelle-like core. Repeated wet–dry cycles then drive these small molecules to organize more strongly, producing a larger, more ordered central assembly.\n\nAs this inner aggregate forms, it seems to recruit or interact with the surrounding phospholipid membrane, leading to a composite structure in which the amphiphile-rich center is enclosed by a mixed phospholipid shell. The right panel emphasizes the vesicle architecture: a circular membrane made of alternating blue and red phospholipids surrounds an interior where free amphiphiles move inward and associate with the central cluster. Arrows suggest ongoing exchange or incorporation of amphiphiles into the inner assembly.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a stepwise self-assembly process in which simple chiral amphiphiles and phospholipids cooperate, with solvent conditions and wet–dry cycling promoting formation of a compartment containing an organized interior aggregate.",
